  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What powers a funicular?",
      "a": "An electric motor in the top station turns the big pulley that moves the cable. Some early funiculars used water instead: the top car filled a tank to become heavier and pulled the other car up.",
      "asks": [
        "How did water-powered funiculars work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the top pulley do?",
      "a": "The cable from one car runs over the pulley and down to the other car. Turning the pulley moves both cars at once, in opposite directions.",
      "asks": [
        "How are funicular cars connected?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do the two cars balance each other?",
      "a": "Both cars weigh about the same, so the one going down pulls the one going up through the cable. The motor only needs to lift the difference in passengers and overcome friction.",
      "asks": [
        "How is a funicular like a seesaw?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do the cars pass each other on one track?",
      "a": "Halfway, the track splits into a passing loop. Each car has double-flanged wheels on one side and wide flat wheels on the other, so it always follows its own side of the loop.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an Abt switch?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens if a funicular cable breaks?",
      "a": "Funicular cars have strong emergency brakes that clamp onto the rails if the cable goes slack or the car goes too fast. The cables are also inspected very often.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a funicular safe?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Stoosbahn in Switzerland climbs a gradient of up to 110 percent, steeper than 45 degrees.",
    "The passing loop used by most funiculars is called an Abt switch, after Swiss engineer Carl Roman Abt.",
    "Some early funiculars were powered by water tanks in the cars instead of motors."
  ],
